<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Manuel III of Trebizond</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1364">1364</a>–<a href="/years/1417">1417</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Manuel III was Emperor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trabzon">Trebizond</a> from 1390 to 1417, the son of Alexios III. Faced with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timur">Timur</a>'s advance into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>, he accepted Timur's overlordship, and after Timur defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ankara">Ankara</a> in 1402 Trebizond enjoyed a respite from Ottoman pressure. He was succeeded by his son Alexios IV.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/manuel-iii-of-trebizond.jpg" alt="Manuel III of Trebizond" width="320" height="269" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CNG Coins · CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Manuel_III_of_Trebizond_coin.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Manuel Megas Komnenos was born on 16 December 1364, the son of Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexios-iii-of-trebizond">Alexios III of Trebizond</a> and Theodora Kantakouzene, and became heir after the death of his elder brother Basil in 1377. That year he married Gulkhan-Eudokia of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a>, a daughter of King David IX of Georgia and the widow of his half-brother Andronikos; their sons included Alexios and Theodore. In 1395 he married Anna Philanthropene of the Byzantine Doukas family. He became emperor on 20 March 1390.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>In 1398 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayezid-i">Sultan Bayezid I</a> led an army along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a> coast to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trabzon">Trebizond</a>'s border. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timur">Timur</a> returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a> and took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sivas">Sivas</a> on 27 August 1400, killing all its defenders. Manuel avoided Timur's demand that he and his army join the war on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a>, though he sent twenty <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galley">galleys</a>. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ankara">Ankara</a>, Timur plundered Ottoman lands for eight months, and the Ottomans were not a threat again until 1413. On leaving in 1403, part of his army ravaged the country around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giresun">Kerasous</a>, and Timur put his nephew Mirza Halil in charge of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a>, Trebizond and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a>.</p><p>Ruy Gonzáles de Clavijo, passing through in April 1404 on his embassy to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timur">Timur</a>, described Manuel and his son in tall hats crowned with cranes' feathers and wrote that the emperor paid tribute to Timur and to other Turks. Manuel renewed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a>'s privileges in 1391 and in 1396 granted the Venetians a church, bank and law court of their own, while his quarrels with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genoa">Genoa</a> over its castle at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trabzon">Trebizond</a> grew serious by 1416.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>His last years were troubled by his son Alexios IV. When nobles angered by the favour shown to a page of humble birth rose with Alexios and besieged Manuel in the upper citadel, he banished the favourite, and father and son were reconciled. Manuel died on 5 March 1417; George Finlay records the rumour that Alexios was suspected of hastening his death. In the year of his accession he had given the Soumela Monastery a reliquary cross believed to hold a piece of the cross on which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a> was crucified.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Manuel III of Trebizond” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Manuel III</b> acknowledged Timur’s overlordship before Timur defeated the Ottomans in 1402, but during the fifteenth century the rise of the Ottoman Empire left Trebizond increasingly isolated.</blockquote><a href="/empire/empire-of-trebizond">Empire of Trebizond · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trabzon">Trabzon</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trabzon">Trabzon</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=41.01,39.72&amp;year=1398" aria-label="Trabzon on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Manuel III of Trebizond"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5624 4038h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5624 4038h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=41.01,39.72&amp;year=1398">What was here in 1398? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
